  { q: 'Is the Morrígan a raven or a crow?', a: 'The Old Irish sources use the word "badb" (which can refer to both crow and raven) and "bran" (crow/raven) for the birds associated with the Morrígan and her aspects. Modern Irish "fiach dubh" means raven; "caróg" means crow. The ancient texts do not always distinguish clearly, and scholars note that the boundary between crow and raven in Old Irish was not drawn as it is in modern ornithology. In practice, the Morrígan is associated with the whole family of large black corvids, with ravens and Hooded Crows both being candidate species in Ireland.' },
  { q: 'Who are the three aspects of the Morrígan?', a: 'The Morrígan is often described as a triad in later sources: the Morrígan proper, Badb (the screaming war-crow), and either Macha or Nemain (or both). The triple structure is not consistent across all early texts. Badb is most closely associated with the battle-crow — hovering over battlefields, screaming, feeding on the slain. The Morrígan in her role as a shape-shifter specifically transforms into bird form in multiple tales.' },
  { q: 'What is the Morrígan\'s role in the Táin Bó Cúailnge?', a: 'In the Táin (the great Irish epic of the cattle-raid of Cooley), the Morrígan interacts with the hero Cú Chulainn in several episodes. She offers him love; when he refuses her, she opposes him — transforming into an eel to trip him, a wolf to scatter cattle at him, and a heifer to cause him harm. Later she appears as an old woman milking a cow and gives him three drinks, healing the wounds she caused. Her relationship with Cú Chulainn is complex: enemy, tester, healer. At his death she perches on his shoulder as a crow.' },
];
